Day 300/300: Determine Whether Matrix Can Be Obtained By Rotation

Problem Statement #1886

Problem Statement Explantion:

Given two n×n binary matrices mat and target, return true if it is possible to make mat equal to target by rotating mat in 90-degree increments (0, 90, 180, or 270 degrees).

Solution Approach:

The most efficient way to rotate a square matrix 90 degrees clockwise in-place involves two linear transformations:
Transpose the Matrix: Convert all rows into columns by swapping elements at mat[i][j] with mat[j][i].
Reverse Each Row: Flip the elements of each row horizontally.
Since there are only four possible orientations, the algorithm:
Compares the current mat with target.
If they don't match, it performs one 90-degree rotation.
Repeats this process up to 4 times. If no match is found after a full 360∘ cycle, the answer is false.

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public boolean findRotation(int[][] mat, int[][] target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            if (isEqual(mat, target)) {
                return true;
            }
            rotate(mat);
        }
        return false;
    }

    private void rotate(int[][] mat) {
        int n = mat.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int temp = mat[i][j];
                mat[i][j] = mat[j][i];
                mat[j][i] = temp;
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int left = 0, right = n - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                int temp = mat[i][left];
                mat[i][left] = mat[i][right];
                mat[i][right] = temp;
                left++;
                right--;
            }
        }
    }

    private boolean isEqual(int[][] mat, int[][] target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < mat.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < mat[0].length; j++) {
                if (mat[i][j] != target[i][j]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(n^2)
Space Complexity: O(1)




